From: gtk-gnutella project <https://github.com/Zero3K20/gtk-gnutella>
Date: 2026-03-25
Subject: [PATCH] [NTOSKRNL/MM] Map rebased images top-down to avoid low address-space fragmentation

ROOT CAUSE
----------
When a PE image (DLL) is mapped via NtMapViewOfSection and its preferred
load address is already occupied, MmMapViewOfSection calls MmFindGap with
TopDown=FALSE.  That performs a strict bottom-up address-space scan and
places the rebased DLL at the lowest available gap.

Because ReactOS does not pre-assign unique preferred base addresses to all
system DLLs at build time (the way Windows NT's linker/rebaser toolchain
does), virtually every DLL in a GTK+/GLib application is rebased at load
time.  Dozens of DLLs are therefore scattered across the low portion of
the user address space, leaving only small, fragmented gaps there.

The cascade of failures this triggers:
  1. A subsequent VirtualAlloc(NULL, large_size, MEM_RESERVE, ...) call
     cannot find a large contiguous gap in the bottom of the address space.
  2. MiInsertVadEx → MiFindEmptyAddressRangeInTree returns STATUS_NO_MEMORY.
  3. The caller receives a NULL pointer.
  4. VirtualFree(NULL) logs "Unable to find VAD for address 0x00000000".
  5. Stack guard-page extension also fails → "Close to our death..." →
     MDL probe crash.

THE FIX
-------
Change the MmFindGap call in the fallback path of MmMapViewOfSection from
bottom-up (FALSE) to top-down (TRUE).  When a DLL must be placed at a
different address than its preferred base, it is now allocated from the
high end of the user address space.  The top of the address space is
almost always completely empty (no heap, no stack, no explicitly placed
allocations), so the rebased DLLs pack neatly there and leave the lower
address space intact for VirtualAlloc, heap, and stack allocations.

This matches the effective behaviour of Windows NT: system DLLs on NT
have non-conflicting preferred addresses so they never need to be rebased,
but when an application DLL is rebased (e.g., because of a conflict) NT's
loader places it in the high part of the address space.

The change is a single-character (FALSE → TRUE) edit in the MmMapViewOfSection
fallback branch.  It only fires when the preferred address is already in use
(NotAtBase == TRUE path), so DLLs that load cleanly at their preferred
address are completely unaffected.

Affected file: ntoskrnl/mm/section.c

diff --git a/ntoskrnl/mm/section.c b/ntoskrnl/mm/section.c
--- a/ntoskrnl/mm/section.c
+++ b/ntoskrnl/mm/section.c
@@ -4130,11 +4130,14 @@ MmMapViewOfSection(IN PVOID SectionObject,
         if (!MmIsAddressRangeFree(AddressSpace, (PVOID)ImageBase, PAGE_ROUND_UP(ImageSize)))
         {
             /* Fail if the user requested a fixed base address. */
             if ((*BaseAddress) != NULL)
             {
                 Status = STATUS_CONFLICTING_ADDRESSES;
                 goto Exit;
             }
-            /* Otherwise find a gap to map the image. */
-            ImageBase = (ULONG_PTR)MmFindGap(AddressSpace, PAGE_ROUND_UP(ImageSize), MM_VIRTMEM_GRANULARITY, FALSE);
+            /*
+             * Otherwise find a gap to map the image.  Search top-down so
+             * that rebased DLLs are placed in the high portion of the user
+             * address space and do not fragment the low address space where
+             * VirtualAlloc(NULL, ...) and heap/stack allocations live.
+             */
+            ImageBase = (ULONG_PTR)MmFindGap(AddressSpace, PAGE_ROUND_UP(ImageSize), MM_VIRTMEM_GRANULARITY, TRUE);
             if (ImageBase == 0)
             {
                 Status = STATUS_CONFLICTING_ADDRESSES;
